      "text": "Logic:\n\n-> LOGIC is a formal system to reason and analyse and deduce statements.\n-> Logic is the language of mathematics and computers. Natural Language like english can feel ambiguous at times. \n   Logic gives precise meaning to statements.\n-> Logic is the basis of all mathematical reasoning and automated reasoning.\n-> There are hundreds of types of logics. We study Propositional and First-Order logic.\n\n\nPropositional Logic:\n\n-> Proposition: is a declarative sentence (that declares a fact) that is either true or false, but not both. i.e. we can assign values True or False to it.\n-> Propositional Variables/Sentential Variables: that take only two values either True or False.\n-> The assertion \"x + 2 = 5\" is not a proposition although it is declaring a fact. But it is not specifying the value/s of x. The variable x is called \"free variable\".\n    If the assertion were to include say, \"X is an integer\" or \"x is 10\", then the statement is a proposition and x is no longer a \"free variable\".\n-> Each proposition is represented by a Propositional variable.\n\n-> \"This sentence is false\". This is not a proposition, it is a \"PARADOX\". \n-> It is not clear if we should assign True or False to it. \n    - If we consider that this statement is True, Then the semtence is stating that it is false. But if it is false,\n      then we cannot assign it true.\n    - If we consider that this statement is False, Then what the sentence is stating is false, which means the sentence is true, so we cannot assign it false.\n\n\nAtomic Proposition: is a proposition whose trueity or falseity doesn't depend on the trueity or falseity of another proposition. They are either true or false by itself.\n-> We can form new propositions from atomic propositions by using logical connectives/operators.\nThese new propositions which are a composition of other propositions are called \"Compound Propositions\". \n\nTruth Table: tells us the truth value of a compound proposition for each combination of truth values of the atomic propositions.\n\n\nLogical Connectives (operators):\n\n1) Negation of a Proposition (NOT operator): If \"p\" is a proposition, then negation of p, is another proposition that says \"it is not the case that p\".\n   eg: p = This book is interesting.   ~p = It is not the case that this book is interesting (or) This book is not interesting. \n\n  - Negation is a unary operator. It acts only on one proposition. If p is true, then ~p is false. If p is false, then ~p is true.\n\n2) Conjunction (AND operator): If \"p\" and \"q\" are two propositions, conjunction forms a new proposition, \"p AND q\" or \"p n q\". \n    \"p AND q\" is true iff p is true and q is true.\n\n    - The words But, Although, though, even though, howerver, yet, neverthless, Still, Moreover, However and comma(,) also represent conjuction.\n\n3) Disjunction (OR operator): If \"p\" and \"q\" are two propositions, disjunction forms a new proposition, \"p OR q\" or \"p v q\".\n    \"p OR a\" is true iff atleast one of them is true.\n\n4) Exclusive OR (XOR operator):\n    - The Disnjunction(OR) is also called \"Inclusive OR\". This is because in addition to the cases when only one of the propositions is true, \n      it also includes the case when both the propositions are true for the proposition to be true.\n    - Exclusive OR doesn't include the last case. If \"p\" and \"q\" are two propositions, then \"p XOR q\" or \"p ⊕ q\" is true iff only one of them is true.\n\n5) NAND: is the negation of AND operator. (p ↑ q) = ~(p AND q) = ~p OR ~q. This is because, the opposite of p is true and q is true is atleast one of them is false.\n\n6) NOR: is the negation of OR operator. (p ↓ q) = ~(p OR q) = ~p AND ~q. This is because, the oppositive of atleast one of them is true is both of them are false.\n\n7) Implication: \n    \n    Consider the statement: If you study well then you will crack the exam.\n    - Here, \"You study well is the condition/antecedent/hypothesis/Premise\n    - \"You will crack the exam\" is the consequence/conclusion.\n    - This can be understood as, If the premise of studying well is fulfilled, I promise you, you will crack the exam.\n    - But, if you don't study well, i don't guarantee anything. you may crack or you may not.\n    - Studying well is IMPLYING cracking the exam.\n    \n    The compound proposition, p -> q, is the implication statement. It is false, only when p is true and q is false. This is because p is promising q, but if p itself is not true\n    it is not obligated to fulfill it's promise. Conditional statements are one way. Only p is being held accountable. \n    \n    So, this is like saying, happening of p is sufficient for q. But happening of q is necessary for p, because it promised.\n    p -> q is equivalent to ~q -> ~p\n\n8) Bi-Implication: p <-> q, is the proposition that states p is necessary and sufficient for q or the vice-versa. They are the iff statements.\n    They are the Definition statements. They are two way.\n    \n    - Implication tell us about the property. Bi-Implication tells us the definition.\n    - P -> Q: Q is a property of P.   P <-> Q:  P and Q are definitions of each other.\n\nPrecdence of Logical connectives: Negation > AND > OR > Implication > Bi-Implication\n\nPROPOSITIONAL VARIABLES ARE ONLY FOR REPRESENTING ATOMIC PROPOSITIONS. COMPOUND PROPOSITIONS ARE PROPOSITIONAL FORMULAE. \nTHEY ARE COMPOSED OF PROPOSITIONAL VARIABLES, TRUTH VALUES AND LOGICAL CONNECTIVES.\n\n-> The meaning of a propositional formula can only be defined when the values of all of its variables are given. Each variable can be true or false.",
