      "text": "Tautology, Contradiction, Contingency and Logical Equivalency\n\n-> Tautology = Always True = Valid = Satisfiable = Unfalsifiable  (Tautalogy is for compound propositions and only cares about truth values of propositional variables. The structure of propsoitional variables decide)\n-> Contradiction = Always False = Fallacy = Unsatisfiable = Invalid = falsifiable   (Same as above)\n-> Contingency = Sometimes True = Satisfiable = Invalid = falsifiable  (Same as above)\n\nSatisfiable: it is possible to make the expression true in atleast one case\nFalsifiable: it is possible to make the expression false in atleast one case\n\n-> Two propositions, p and q are equal or logically equivalent if they always have the same truth value. i.e. for \n    p is true whenever q is true and vice-versa, and p is false whenever q is false and vice-versa.\n\n-> Two compound propositions A and B are logically equivalent iff A and B have same truth tables. i.e. the propositional variables must be same and each of the combination of truth values\n    must yeild same result. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Tautology, Contradiction, Contingency and Logical Equivalency\n\n-> Tautology = Always True = Valid = Satisfiable = Unfalsifiable  (Tautalogy is for compound propositions and only cares about truth values of propositional variables. The structure of propsoitional variables decide)\n-> Contradiction = Always False = Fallacy = Unsatisfiable = Invalid = falsifiable   (Same as above)\n-> Contingency = Sometimes True = Satisfiable = Invalid = falsifiable  (Same as above)\n\nSatisfiable: it is possible to make the expression true in atleast one case\nFalsifiable: it is possible to make the expression false in atleast one case\n\n-> Two propositions, p and q are equal or logically equivalent if they always have the same truth value. i.e. for \n    p is true whenever q is true and vice-versa, and p is false whenever q is false and vice-versa.\n\n-> Two compound propositions A and B are logically equivalent iff A and B have same truth tables. i.e. the propositional variables must be same and each of the combination of truth values\n    must yeild same result. ",
