      "locked": false,
      "text": "Converse, Inverse, Contrapositive of Conditional Statements:\n\n-> Given p -> q, \n    - Converse: q -> p\n    - Contrapositive: ~q -> ~p\n    - Inverse: ~p -> ~q\n\n-> For any conditional statement, Implication is equivalent to Contrapositive of Implication.\n\nArguments: Arguments are a sequence of premises/propositions/forumlae and a conclusion/Inference derived from it.\n-> If all the preimises and the conclusion are true, then it is a valid argument. If not, Invalid argument.\n-> That means if A, B, C are propositions and D is a conclusion. Then, A n B n C => D. must be a tautology.\n\nArgument form is a sequence of compound propositions involving propositional variables. An argument form is valid no matter which\nparticular propositions are substituted for the propositional variables in its premises, the conclusion is true if the premises are all true.\n\n\nFORMULAE FOR LAWS, PROPERTIES, LOGICAL EQUIVALENCIES AND RULES OF INFERENCE.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Converse, Inverse, Contrapositive of Conditional Statements:\n\n-> Given p -> q, \n    - Converse: q -> p\n    - Contrapositive: ~q -> ~p\n    - Inverse: ~p -> ~q\n\n-> For any conditional statement, Implication is equivalent to Contrapositive of Implication.\n\nArguments: Arguments are a sequence of premises/propositions/forumlae and a conclusion/Inference derived from it.\n-> If all the preimises and the conclusion are true, then it is a valid argument. If not, Invalid argument.\n-> That means if A, B, C are propositions and D is a conclusion. Then, A n B n C => D. must be a tautology.\n\nArgument form is a sequence of compound propositions involving propositional variables. An argument form is valid no matter which\nparticular propositions are substituted for the propositional variables in its premises, the conclusion is true if the premises are all true.\n\n\nFORMULAE FOR LAWS, PROPERTIES, LOGICAL EQUIVALENCIES AND RULES OF INFERENCE.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
