      "locked": false,
      "text": "Proof Techniques:\n\n-> Conjecture: An open statement. A statement that is neither proven nor disproven.\n-> Axioms: Statements that are always true. An unprovable rule or first principle accepted as true because it is self-evident. \n-> Definitions: Used to create new concepts from existing ones. A proposition.\n-> Theorem: A proposition that has been proved to be true.\n\n\nMethods to prove:\n\n1) Direct Proof: To prove \"If P then Q\", start with P, apply facts that you know are true and can be derived from P. Derive Q.\n   - Assume P => Logical Deductions => Therefore Q.\n\nExamples:\n-> If m and n are perfect squares, then m*n is also a perfect square:\n\n    Let m = a^2 and n = b^2, where a, b are integers.\n    => m*n = (ab)^2, ab is an integer.\n    Hence, m*n is a perfect square.\n\n-> If 5|2a (5 divides 2a), then 5|a\n\n   2a = 5k, where k is an integer.\n   even = even\n   this means, 5k must be even.\n   => 2a = 5(2b), where b is an integer\n   => a = 5b\n   Hence, 5 divides a.\n\n\n2) Proof by Contraposition: To prove \"If P then Q\", prove \"If not Q, then not P\".\n    - Assume Q is false => Logical Deductions => Conclude P is also false.\n\nExamples: \n-> Prove that if n is an integer and n^2 is even, then n is also even.\n    \n    - Direct Proof:\n      n^2 = 2k\n      n = sqrt(2k) \n        ..... We are stuck here, there is no way to proceed. \n      It is sometimes not possible to prove something with just one method. we need to understand the limitations and try different methods.\n\n    - Proof by contraposition:\n      Assume n is odd. (Q is false)\n      n = 2k + 1\n      n^2 = (4k^2 + 4k) + 1\n      Hence, n^2 is odd. (P Is also false).\n\n-> If r^2 is irrational, then r is irrational\n    \n    Assume r is rational (Q is false)\n    => r = a/b, where a,b are integers\n    => r^2 = a^2/b^2\n    Hence r^2 is also a rational (P is false)\n\nRational numbers can be recurring, but with a fixed pattern. you can find the fraction, by doing, 10^k * decimal - decimal.\nRational numbers are those which can be represented as a fraction of integers, where it's lowest form has a gcd(numerator, denominator) is 1.\n\n\n3) Proof by contradiction: To prove \"P is true\", if you can prove \"P is false\" is impossible, then P must be true.\n    Prove that \"not P\" leads to a ridiculous result, so P must be true.\n\nExamples:\n-> Prove that Sqrt(2) is irrational\n\n    Assume sqrt(2) is rational.\n    sqrt(2) = a/b\n    2 = a^2/b^2\n    a^2 = 2 * b^2\n    (2c)^2 = 2 * b^2     ...Since, a^2 is even, then a must also be even.\n    4c^2 = 2b^2\n    2C = b^2               ...Since, b^2 is even, then b must also be even.\n    \n    Thus, a and b have a gcd of 2, which shouldn't be the case, if a/b is in lowest rational form.\n    Hence, sqrt(2) is irrational.\n\n-> Prove that there is no largest integer\n    \n    Assume a is the largest integer.\n    But, a + 1 > a. \n    Hence, a is not largest integer. There is no largest integer.\n\n-> If a,b are integers, Prove that a^2 - 4b != 2\n    \n    Assume, a^2 - 4b = 2\n               a^2 = 2(b + 1)\n               a = sqrt(2) * sqrt(b + 1)\n    irrational number multiplied anything is also an irrational number,\n    But it is given that a is an integer. This is a contradiction.\n    Hence given expression is true.\n    \n\n4) Disprove by counter example: We can show that P is false, by giving an example where P is false. \n\n5) Proof by Induction: Given a function P(n), where n takes values which can be arranged in a well defined order. Then\n    we need to show that:\n    - P(n) is true for the lowest possible value n can take. \n    - For any k, that n can take, P(k) is true if there is some sort of relation between P(k) and one or more terms of P(x) where x takes values less than k \n      and if P is true for all x.\n\n    Here, the comparison or relatedness is not based on the magnitude of values that n can take. It is based on the property that defines an order.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Proof Techniques:\n\n-> Conjecture: An open statement. A statement that is neither proven nor disproven.\n-> Axioms: Statements that are always true. An unprovable rule or first principle accepted as true because it is self-evident. \n-> Definitions: Used to create new concepts from existing ones. A proposition.\n-> Theorem: A proposition that has been proved to be true.\n\n\nMethods to prove:\n\n1) Direct Proof: To prove \"If P then Q\", start with P, apply facts that you know are true and can be derived from P. Derive Q.\n   - Assume P => Logical Deductions => Therefore Q.\n\nExamples:\n-> If m and n are perfect squares, then m*n is also a perfect square:\n\n    Let m = a^2 and n = b^2, where a, b are integers.\n    => m*n = (ab)^2, ab is an integer.\n    Hence, m*n is a perfect square.\n\n-> If 5|2a (5 divides 2a), then 5|a\n\n   2a = 5k, where k is an integer.\n   even = even\n   this means, 5k must be even.\n   => 2a = 5(2b), where b is an integer\n   => a = 5b\n   Hence, 5 divides a.\n\n\n2) Proof by Contraposition: To prove \"If P then Q\", prove \"If not Q, then not P\".\n    - Assume Q is false => Logical Deductions => Conclude P is also false.\n\nExamples: \n-> Prove that if n is an integer and n^2 is even, then n is also even.\n    \n    - Direct Proof:\n      n^2 = 2k\n      n = sqrt(2k) \n        ..... We are stuck here, there is no way to proceed. \n      It is sometimes not possible to prove something with just one method. we need to understand the limitations and try different methods.\n\n    - Proof by contraposition:\n      Assume n is odd. (Q is false)\n      n = 2k + 1\n      n^2 = (4k^2 + 4k) + 1\n      Hence, n^2 is odd. (P Is also false).\n\n-> If r^2 is irrational, then r is irrational\n    \n    Assume r is rational (Q is false)\n    => r = a/b, where a,b are integers\n    => r^2 = a^2/b^2\n    Hence r^2 is also a rational (P is false)\n\nRational numbers can be recurring, but with a fixed pattern. you can find the fraction, by doing, 10^k * decimal - decimal.\nRational numbers are those which can be represented as a fraction of integers, where it's lowest form has a gcd(numerator, denominator) is 1.\n\n\n3) Proof by contradiction: To prove \"P is true\", if you can prove \"P is false\" is impossible, then P must be true.\n    Prove that \"not P\" leads to a ridiculous result, so P must be true.\n\nExamples:\n-> Prove that Sqrt(2) is irrational\n\n    Assume sqrt(2) is rational.\n    sqrt(2) = a/b\n    2 = a^2/b^2\n    a^2 = 2 * b^2\n    (2c)^2 = 2 * b^2     ...Since, a^2 is even, then a must also be even.\n    4c^2 = 2b^2\n    2C = b^2               ...Since, b^2 is even, then b must also be even.\n    \n    Thus, a and b have a gcd of 2, which shouldn't be the case, if a/b is in lowest rational form.\n    Hence, sqrt(2) is irrational.\n\n-> Prove that there is no largest integer\n    \n    Assume a is the largest integer.\n    But, a + 1 > a. \n    Hence, a is not largest integer. There is no largest integer.\n\n-> If a,b are integers, Prove that a^2 - 4b != 2\n    \n    Assume, a^2 - 4b = 2\n               a^2 = 2(b + 1)\n               a = sqrt(2) * sqrt(b + 1)\n    irrational number multiplied anything is also an irrational number,\n    But it is given that a is an integer. This is a contradiction.\n    Hence given expression is true.\n    \n\n4) Disprove by counter example: We can show that P is false, by giving an example where P is false. \n\n5) Proof by Induction: Given a function P(n), where n takes values which can be arranged in a well defined order. Then\n    we need to show that:\n    - P(n) is true for the lowest possible value n can take. \n    - For any k, that n can take, P(k) is true if there is some sort of relation between P(k) and one or more terms of P(x) where x takes values less than k \n      and if P is true for all x.\n\n    Here, the comparison or relatedness is not based on the magnitude of values that n can take. It is based on the property that defines an order.",
