      "x": 2653.6392500475895,
      "y": 48034.14878985766,
      "width": 2261.637939453125,
      "height": 4100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bAq",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 995467916,
      "version": 10722,
      "versionNonce": 1602498356,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787048915036,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is the need of First-Order-Logic?\n\n-> Propositional logic only consists of Propositional variables. Meaning it only evaluates Truth Values and outputs Truth Values.\n-> What if you want to evaluate it over a set of objects? And what if you are not just evaluating an assertion over these objects, but also the properties they satisfy,\n    The relations that these objects have among themselves, And whether do all of them or some of them satisfy a property, And the transformations of these objects?\n-> That's where FOL comes into play. Propositional logic assumes there are facts that are either true or false. FOL assumes that the world consists of objects\n    with certain relations among them that are true or false.\n\nObjects, Domain, Predicates\n\n-> FOL speaks about objects. Each variable refers to some object.\n-> The set of objects that we discuss is called the \"Domain\" or \"Universe\" or \"Domain of discourse\".\n\n-> Predicate tells us about the properties of objects and relationship among objects.\n-> Example: If Domain is set of People, Then \"Male(x)\" will be evaluated to true or false. Here, \"Male\" is the predicate and x is the variables that takes a person's name.\n-> Example: F(x, y), The predicate F represents a relation among x,y. i.e. whether x is Father of y. \n-> Predicate is a sentence containing variables of objects. It becomes a proposition when the variables are substituted with objects from domain.\n-> Predicates take objects as arguments and evaluate to True or False. So, they are also called \"Propositional Functions\".\n\nNote: Domain is always non-empty, unless it is explicitly stated.\nNote: If nothing is mentioned about domain or domain of different variables, Then all variables shall have the same domain called \"Domain/Universe of the expression\".\n\n\nWe can convert Predicate into proposition by substituting object variables with values. There is another way to do it. We can talk about the predicate from the perspective of\nmultiple objects of domain. i.e. we QUANTIFY the predicate/property/relation to some extent of the domain. We talk about the satisfaction of property by Multiple Objects.\n\nQuantifiers:\n\n-> In FOL, there are only two quantifiers we discuss.\n\n1) Universal Quantifier:\n\n- The Universal Quantifier checks if the property is satisfied by ALL the objects in the domain.\n- It is true only when \"For all x, P(x)\" is true. Represented by, \"∀x P(x)\"\n- The universal quantification of P(x) is the proposition, \"P(x) is true for all values x in the universe of discourse\"\n- An elements for which P(x) is false is called a \"Counterexample\" of ∀x P(x).\n- If the domain is finite, Then Universal quantification is just the conjunction of Predicate Instantiations with every object in the domain.\n\n\n2) Existential Quantifier:\n\n- The Existential Quantifier checks if the property is satisfied by atleast one object in the domain.\n- It is true only when \"For some x, P(x)\" is true. Represented by \"∃x P(x)\"\n- The Existential Quantification of P(x) is the proposition, \"There exisits an x in the domain, for which P(x) is true\".\n- If the domain is finite, then Existential quantification is just the disjunction of Predicate Instantiations with every object in the domain.\n\nNote: When the domain is stated to be empty, Then Universal Quantification is always true and Existential quantification is always false.\nNote: Predicate with no variable or no free variable is a proposition. Then \"For all x, P = P\" and \"There exists an x, P = P\"\n\n\nFree Variables and Bounded Variables\n\n-> In a FOL expression, Free variables are those that can take any value from the domain.\n-> Bounded variables are those that are bounded by a quantifier. i.e. they can't take a random variable. the quantifier already locked that variable\n    for evaluation.\n-> An expression with no free variables is a Proposition. A quantified predicate or a literl propositional statement.\n-> An expression with free variables is a Predicate. let X and Y be different domains then, For all x (x > y) has a free variable y.\n-> A variable is free if it is not bounded by a quantifier.\n\n\nScope of a quantifier:\n\n- The part of a logical expression to which a quantifier is applied is called the scope of this quantifier.\n- The scope of a quantifier in a formula P is the quantifier itself and the subformula that immediately follows the quantifier.\n\n\nNested Quantifiers:\n\n- A quantifier is nested if it is within the scope of another quantifier.\n- Quantifiers must be read from left to right.\n- Think of nested quantifiers as nested loops.\n\n\nNegation of quantifiers:\n\n-> m = For all X, P(x),   ~m = There exisits an x, ~P(x)\n-> m = There exists an x, P(x)   ~m = For all x, ~P(x)\n-> For expressions involving nested quantifiers, Just evaluate the subformulae one by one, applying the negation at\n    the outer quantifiers and then moving the negation inside until you reach the inner most predicate and negate it.\n\nValidity and Satisfiability of FOL expressions:\n\n- Validity means always True. For any non-empty domain and any predicate we take.\n- Satisfiability means Possible to be True. For some domain and some predicate.\n\n- To check validity or satisfiability of a FOL expression, just analyse what the expression is saying, and try to verify it for some abstract domain and predicate.\n- For validity, check if there is no combination of domain and predicate exists that can make the expression false.\n- For satisfiablity, check if there is some combination of domain and predicate exists that can make the expression true.\n- In all kinds of cases, if an expression is valid, Then the expression is something very obvious from the perspective of common logic\n    and if it is not satisfiable, then the expression itself is ridiculous.\n\n\nLogical Equivalence of FOL expressions:\n\n- Two expressions are logically equivalent iff they have same truth value no matter which predicates are substituted and which domains are used.\n   i.e. expr1 and expr2 must be valid. i.e. expr1 <-> expr2 must be true for any combination of domain and prediate at a given time.\n\nDistributing Quantifiers over logical connectives:\n\n1) AND\n\n- For all x (P(x) AND Q(x))  <->  For all x P(x)  AND  For all y Q(y)\n- TE an x (P(x) AND Q(x))   ->   TE an x P(x) AND  TE a y Q(y) \n\n2) OR\n\n- For all x (P(x) OR Q(x))  <-  For all x P(x)  OR  For all y Q(y)\n- TE an x (P(x) or Q(x))  <->  TE an x P(x)  OR TE a y Q(y)\n\n3) XOR\n\n- TE an x (P(x) XOR Q(x))  <-  TE an x P(x)  XOR  TE a y Q(y)\n\n4) Implication\n\n- For all x (P(x) -> Q(x))  ->  ( For all x P(x) -> For all y Q(y) )\n- TE an x (P(x) -> Q(x))  <-  ( TE an x P(x) -> TE a y Q(y) ) \n\n5) Bi-Implication\n\n- For all x (P(x) <-> Q(x))  ->  ( For all x P(x)  <-> For all y Q(y) )\n\n6) NAND\n\n- For all x (P(x) NAND Q(x))  ->  For all x P(x) NAND For all y Q(y)\n- TE an x (P(x) NAND Q(x))  <-  TE an x P(x) NAND TE a y Q(y)\n\n7) NOR\n\n- For all x (P(x) NOR Q(x))  ->  For all x P(x) NOR For all y Q(y)\n- TE an x (P(x) NOR Q(x))  <-  TE an x P(x) NOR TE a y Q(y)\n\n\nNull Quantification:\n\n- Null quantification occurs when a quantifier is applied to an expression or sub-expression that doesn't contain x as a free variable.\n  Because x does not appear inside that scope, the quantifier performs no work and can either be ddropped entirely or moved across logical connectives.\n\nFor all x, C = C\n\nTE an x, C = C\n\nFor all x (P(x) AND C) = For all x P(x)  AND  C\n\nTE an x (P(x) AND C) = TE an x P(x)  AND  C\n\nFor all x (P(x) OR C)  =  For all x P(x)  OR C\n\nTE an x (P(x) OR C)  = TE an x P(x) OR C\n\nFor all x (C -> P(x))   =  C -> For all x P(x)\n\nTE an x (C -> P(x))   =  C -> TE an x P(x)\n\nFor all x (P(x) -> C)   =  TE an x P(x) -> C\n\nTE an x (P(x) -> C)   =  For all x P(x) -> C\n\n\nTautology in FOL: You have to replace the sub-expressions by a variables and negation of those variables, wherever applicable. If the resultant COMPOUND PROPOSITION is a tautology, then the expression is a tautology in FOL.\n\n\nRULES OF INFERENCE IN FOL\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is the need of First-Order-Logic?\n\n-> Propositional logic only consists of Propositional variables. Meaning it only evaluates Truth Values and outputs Truth Values.\n-> What if you want to evaluate it over a set of objects? And what if you are not just evaluating an assertion over these objects, but also the properties they satisfy,\n    The relations that these objects have among themselves, And whether do all of them or some of them satisfy a property, And the transformations of these objects?\n-> That's where FOL comes into play. Propositional logic assumes there are facts that are either true or false. FOL assumes that the world consists of objects\n    with certain relations among them that are true or false.\n\nObjects, Domain, Predicates\n\n-> FOL speaks about objects. Each variable refers to some object.\n-> The set of objects that we discuss is called the \"Domain\" or \"Universe\" or \"Domain of discourse\".\n\n-> Predicate tells us about the properties of objects and relationship among objects.\n-> Example: If Domain is set of People, Then \"Male(x)\" will be evaluated to true or false. Here, \"Male\" is the predicate and x is the variables that takes a person's name.\n-> Example: F(x, y), The predicate F represents a relation among x,y. i.e. whether x is Father of y. \n-> Predicate is a sentence containing variables of objects. It becomes a proposition when the variables are substituted with objects from domain.\n-> Predicates take objects as arguments and evaluate to True or False. So, they are also called \"Propositional Functions\".\n\nNote: Domain is always non-empty, unless it is explicitly stated.\nNote: If nothing is mentioned about domain or domain of different variables, Then all variables shall have the same domain called \"Domain/Universe of the expression\".\n\n\nWe can convert Predicate into proposition by substituting object variables with values. There is another way to do it. We can talk about the predicate from the perspective of\nmultiple objects of domain. i.e. we QUANTIFY the predicate/property/relation to some extent of the domain. We talk about the satisfaction of property by Multiple Objects.\n\nQuantifiers:\n\n-> In FOL, there are only two quantifiers we discuss.\n\n1) Universal Quantifier:\n\n- The Universal Quantifier checks if the property is satisfied by ALL the objects in the domain.\n- It is true only when \"For all x, P(x)\" is true. Represented by, \"∀x P(x)\"\n- The universal quantification of P(x) is the proposition, \"P(x) is true for all values x in the universe of discourse\"\n- An elements for which P(x) is false is called a \"Counterexample\" of ∀x P(x).\n- If the domain is finite, Then Universal quantification is just the conjunction of Predicate Instantiations with every object in the domain.\n\n\n2) Existential Quantifier:\n\n- The Existential Quantifier checks if the property is satisfied by atleast one object in the domain.\n- It is true only when \"For some x, P(x)\" is true. Represented by \"∃x P(x)\"\n- The Existential Quantification of P(x) is the proposition, \"There exisits an x in the domain, for which P(x) is true\".\n- If the domain is finite, then Existential quantification is just the disjunction of Predicate Instantiations with every object in the domain.\n\nNote: When the domain is stated to be empty, Then Universal Quantification is always true and Existential quantification is always false.\nNote: Predicate with no variable or no free variable is a proposition. Then \"For all x, P = P\" and \"There exists an x, P = P\"\n\n\nFree Variables and Bounded Variables\n\n-> In a FOL expression, Free variables are those that can take any value from the domain.\n-> Bounded variables are those that are bounded by a quantifier. i.e. they can't take a random variable. the quantifier already locked that variable\n    for evaluation.\n-> An expression with no free variables is a Proposition. A quantified predicate or a literl propositional statement.\n-> An expression with free variables is a Predicate. let X and Y be different domains then, For all x (x > y) has a free variable y.\n-> A variable is free if it is not bounded by a quantifier.\n\n\nScope of a quantifier:\n\n- The part of a logical expression to which a quantifier is applied is called the scope of this quantifier.\n- The scope of a quantifier in a formula P is the quantifier itself and the subformula that immediately follows the quantifier.\n\n\nNested Quantifiers:\n\n- A quantifier is nested if it is within the scope of another quantifier.\n- Quantifiers must be read from left to right.\n- Think of nested quantifiers as nested loops.\n\n\nNegation of quantifiers:\n\n-> m = For all X, P(x),   ~m = There exisits an x, ~P(x)\n-> m = There exists an x, P(x)   ~m = For all x, ~P(x)\n-> For expressions involving nested quantifiers, Just evaluate the subformulae one by one, applying the negation at\n    the outer quantifiers and then moving the negation inside until you reach the inner most predicate and negate it.\n\nValidity and Satisfiability of FOL expressions:\n\n- Validity means always True. For any non-empty domain and any predicate we take.\n- Satisfiability means Possible to be True. For some domain and some predicate.\n\n- To check validity or satisfiability of a FOL expression, just analyse what the expression is saying, and try to verify it for some abstract domain and predicate.\n- For validity, check if there is no combination of domain and predicate exists that can make the expression false.\n- For satisfiablity, check if there is some combination of domain and predicate exists that can make the expression true.\n- In all kinds of cases, if an expression is valid, Then the expression is something very obvious from the perspective of common logic\n    and if it is not satisfiable, then the expression itself is ridiculous.\n\n\nLogical Equivalence of FOL expressions:\n\n- Two expressions are logically equivalent iff they have same truth value no matter which predicates are substituted and which domains are used.\n   i.e. expr1 and expr2 must be valid. i.e. expr1 <-> expr2 must be true for any combination of domain and prediate at a given time.\n\nDistributing Quantifiers over logical connectives:\n\n1) AND\n\n- For all x (P(x) AND Q(x))  <->  For all x P(x)  AND  For all y Q(y)\n- TE an x (P(x) AND Q(x))   ->   TE an x P(x) AND  TE a y Q(y) \n\n2) OR\n\n- For all x (P(x) OR Q(x))  <-  For all x P(x)  OR  For all y Q(y)\n- TE an x (P(x) or Q(x))  <->  TE an x P(x)  OR TE a y Q(y)\n\n3) XOR\n\n- TE an x (P(x) XOR Q(x))  <-  TE an x P(x)  XOR  TE a y Q(y)\n\n4) Implication\n\n- For all x (P(x) -> Q(x))  ->  ( For all x P(x) -> For all y Q(y) )\n- TE an x (P(x) -> Q(x))  <-  ( TE an x P(x) -> TE a y Q(y) ) \n\n5) Bi-Implication\n\n- For all x (P(x) <-> Q(x))  ->  ( For all x P(x)  <-> For all y Q(y) )\n\n6) NAND\n\n- For all x (P(x) NAND Q(x))  ->  For all x P(x) NAND For all y Q(y)\n- TE an x (P(x) NAND Q(x))  <-  TE an x P(x) NAND TE a y Q(y)\n\n7) NOR\n\n- For all x (P(x) NOR Q(x))  ->  For all x P(x) NOR For all y Q(y)\n- TE an x (P(x) NOR Q(x))  <-  TE an x P(x) NOR TE a y Q(y)\n\n\nNull Quantification:\n\n- Null quantification occurs when a quantifier is applied to an expression or sub-expression that doesn't contain x as a free variable.\n  Because x does not appear inside that scope, the quantifier performs no work and can either be ddropped entirely or moved across logical connectives.\n\nFor all x, C = C\n\nTE an x, C = C\n\nFor all x (P(x) AND C) = For all x P(x)  AND  C\n\nTE an x (P(x) AND C) = TE an x P(x)  AND  C\n\nFor all x (P(x) OR C)  =  For all x P(x)  OR C\n\nTE an x (P(x) OR C)  = TE an x P(x) OR C\n\nFor all x (C -> P(x))   =  C -> For all x P(x)\n\nTE an x (C -> P(x))   =  C -> TE an x P(x)\n\nFor all x (P(x) -> C)   =  TE an x P(x) -> C\n\nTE an x (P(x) -> C)   =  For all x P(x) -> C\n\n\nTautology in FOL: You have to replace the sub-expressions by a variables and negation of those variables, wherever applicable. If the resultant COMPOUND PROPOSITION is a tautology, then the expression is a tautology in FOL.\n\n\nRULES OF INFERENCE IN FOL\n",
