      "text": "Set Theory and Relations\n\n-> A Set is an unordered collection of distinct elements.\n-> If an element a is an element of set S, then a belongs to S, a E S.\n-> Cardinality of a set is the number of elements it contains.\n-> If the cardinality of a set is a whole number, then the set is finite, otherwise infinite.\n\nSubset: A set S is called a subset of a set T, if all elements of S are also elements of T.\nProper Subset: S is called a subset of a set T, if all elements of S are also elements of T and T has some extra elements.\nPowerset: P(S) is the powerset of set S, if it is the set of all subsets of S. If S has n elements then P(S) has 2^n subsets as elements.\n\n\nOrdered Pair: A sequence of two elements. (a, b). order and repitition matter. eg: co-ordinates in XY co-ordinate system.\nOrdered n-tuple: A sequence of n elements. Order and repitition matter. (1, 2, ... , n)\nSequence: Can be Finite or Infinite. Order and Repitition matter. < a1, a2, ..... > \n\nCartesian Product: of two sets A and B is: A X B = { (a, b) | a E A and b E B }\nA1 X A2 X ... X An = { (a1, a2, ... , an) | ai E Ai }\n\nCardinality of A X B is Cardinality if A times Cardinality of B. |AXB| = |A| * |B|\nA X EmptySet is an Empty Set.\nAXB != BXA\n(AXB)XC != AX(BXC)\n\n\n\nRelation:\n\n-> Relations are defined among Sets. Given Sets A1, A2, ... , An, a subset of A1 X A2 X ... X An, R is an n-ary relation.\n-> Eg: A Table in a relational database, contains tuples which belong to different domains(sets). The table is a relation\n    because it is a subset of cross-product of all the domains of the attributes.\n-> A relation as the name suggests specifies a relation among the elements of it. For example, each tuple in Student table specifies that, \n    \"Student name is of age X in class Y and he resides in City Z\".\n    \nWe primariliy talk about BINARY RELATIONS. The relations whose elements are ordered Pairs. and represent relation between\nelements of two sets.\n\n-> If (a, b) belongs to some Relation R, the we say a is related to b by R, represented by aRb.\n-> Relations are one way, which means a is related to b by R, but not b is related to a by R.\n-> We denote a relation R between two sets by, R : A -> B means R is a subset of A X B\n->So, a complete representation of a binary relation is: { (a, b) | some relation between a and b where a E A and b E B }\n\n-> R : AXB -> BXA, is a subset of (AXB) X (BXA), where each element is of the form ((ai, bi) , (aj, bj))\n-> A binary relation can exist from set A to itself. i.e. R: A -> A, is valid and is a subset of A X A, where each element is of form (ai, aj).\n    This is often called as \"Relation on  set A\". Here A is called the BASE SET.\n-> eg: {(a, b) | a < b where a, b belong to positive even numbers less than 10}\n\n-> Binary Relations from a set to itself can be represented in matrix forms or directed graph forms.\n-> A X A is called Universal relation or Full relation on A.\n\n\nTypes of Relations from a set to itself:\n\n1) Reflexive:\n\n-> If R: A -> A, and |A| = n, then R is reflexive iff (ai, ai) E R for all i upto n.\n-> Every element in the base set is related to itself. for all x E A, xRx\n-> Matrix representation of a reflexive relation SHALL HAVE all diagonal elements as 1.\n-> Digraph representation SHALL HAVE self edges/loops for all nodes.\n\n-> The Relation is IRREFLEXIVE, iff for all x E A, xRx is false.\n-> i.e. No element should be related to itself. There exists an x, xRx.\n-> Matrix Rep SHALL HAVE all zeros in diagonal. Digraph Rep shall have no self loops.\n-> Reflexive and Irreflexive or NOT OPPOSITE.\n\n-> Any relation that is not reflexive can be called as NOT REFLEXIVE.\n-> Matrix Rep SHALL HAVE Atleast one Zero in diagonal. Digraph Rep shall have atleast one node without self loop.\n\n\n2) Symmetric:\n\n-> Let R: A -> A, R is symmetric iff For all a,b (aRb -> bRa) where a,b E A\n-> If the relation says a is related to b, then it must also say b is related to a.\n-> The matrix formed by the representation is a SYMMETRIC MATRIX\n-> Digraph representation shall have ONLY double edges and self loops.\n\n-> a relation is NOT SYMMETRIC, iff TE a,b (aRb AND b~Ra)\n-> matrix Rep is NOT SYMMETRIC. graph rep has atleast one single edge between a pair of nodes.\n\n-> a relation is ANTI SYMMETRIC, iff For all a,b ((aRb AND a != b) -> b~Ra)\n-> i.e. the relation must NOT contain both (a,b) and (b,a) if a != b.\n-> The graph Rep must NOT have any DOUBLE EDGES, but can have self loops. the matrix REP is not symmetric.\n\n-> a relation is ASYMMETRIC if it is both ANTI SYMMETRIC AND IRREFLEXIVE.\n-> i.e. For all a,b (aRb -> b~Ra).\n-> The graph rep MUST NOT HAVE SELF LOOPS AND DOUBLE EDGES. the matrix rep has all zeros in diagonal elements and is NOT SYMMETRIC.\n\n\n3) Transitive Relation:\n\n-> Let R: A -> A, R is transitive iff for all a,b,c ((aRb AND bRc) -> aRc)\n-> i.e. if the relations contains both (a,b) and (b,c) then it must contain (a,c)\n-> Graph rep has this scenario: there is an edge from a to b and b to c and a to c.\n\n\nPartition of a set:\n\n-> A partition of a non-empty set S is a collection of pairwise disjoint non-empty subsets of S that have\n    S as their union.\n\n\nEquivalence Relation:\n\n-> a relation R is equivalence realtion iff it is Reflexive and Symmetric and Transitive.\n-> an Equivalence relation partitions the base set. This is because in an equivalence relation, an element of\n    base set is only related to a certain group of elements. These groups are called EQUIVALENCE CLASSES. and they are\n    the parts of the paritition.\n-> Eg: Let, A = {1, 2, ... , 20}. and aRb iff a is congruent to (b mod 4).\n    Then, the equivalence classes are (1,5,9.13,17) (2,6,10,14,18) (3,7,11,15,19) (4,8.12,16,20)\n    Notice that an element of an equivalent class is only related to the elements of that class.\n    R = (E1 X E1) U (E2 X E2) U ... U (En X En). So, we can also say |A| = sum of squares of cardinalities of each equivalence classes.\n    Remeber that the cardinalities of equivalence classes can be different.\n-> We can also say, that for every partition on a set, there is a unique equivalence relation.\n-> if aRb when R is an equivalence relation, then we say a is equivalent to b or a and b are equivalent elements.\n-> Equivalence class of an element is denoted by [a] or [a] subscript R. if y E [x] then x E [y] and [x] = [y]\n-> # Equivalence Relations on A = # Partitions on A\n\nNOTE: Complete/Full relations are always Equivalence relations. But if cardinaliy of base set is equal to 1, then the \nrelation is also anti-symmetric. And all full relations have only part/equivalence class in the partition.\n\n\n\nParitial Order Relations:\n\n-> We can use relations to ORDER some or all of the elements of a Set.\n-> For example, If S is the set of words, and R is the relation, such that aRb iff a comes before b in a dictionary,\n   Then we can order the words based on the relation defined.\n-> Notice that, such a relation can never be symmetric. The relation must be transitive. And we can add reflexivity to\n    model non-strict ordering. Which results in the relation to also be anti-symmetric.\n\n-> If A is the base set and R is the POR defined on the set, then the combination (A, R) is called \n    \"Partially Ordered Set\" or \"POSET\".\n-> We use the symbol ⪯ for representing POR. So, (A, ⪯) is a poset defined on some arbitrary POR. \n    a ⪯ b represents that aRb by some arbitrary POR.\n-> Two elements of a set are COMPARABLE if aRb or bRa by some arbitrary POR. i.e. a ⪯ b or b ⪯ a.\n-> If all the elements are comparable to each other. Then the relation is called TOTAL ORDER RELATION or LINEAR ORDER or SIMPLE ORDER.\n-> If some of them are not comparable then it is called PARTIAL ORDER RELATION.\n\nexamples of POR: a | b on some set of natural numbers. a is a factor of b on some set of natural numbers.\nexamples of TOR: a <= b on set of natural numbers. a >= b on set of natural numbers. \n\n\nHasse Diagrams:\n\n-> Hasse Diagrams are defined only for POSETs. they are just digraphs with self loops and transitive edge removed, since they are implicit.\n    You can also remove the direction of arrows and assume all directions are upwards.\n\n-> So in a Hasse Diagram, aRb iff there is an upward PATH from a to b. there won't be an edge unless a and b are neighbors.\n    -> If two elements are not part of same path, then they are INCOMPARABLE.\n\n\nMaximal Element: in a POSET is the element M, such that for all a (a != M -> M ~R a). M is at the top of it's path. Paths end at M.\nMinimal Element:  in a POSET is the element m, such that for all a (a != m -> a~Rm). m is at the bottom of it's path. Paths start at m.\n\nGreatest/Maximum Element: For all a (aRG). G is the maximum element. Only One\nLeast/Minimum Element: For all a (LRa). L is the minimum element. Only One\n\nUpper Bound: Let (A, ⪯) be a poset. Defined for a subset of S. If there exisits an element a in A, such that\nsRa for all s in S. Then a is called the Upper Bound of subset S.\n\n-> If TE a (For all s (sRa)), a E A and s E S. S is subset of A, then a is the upper bound of S\n\nLower Bound: Let (A, ⪯) be a poset. Defined for a subset of S. If there exisits an element a in A, such that\naRs for all s in S. Then a is called the Lower Bound of subset S.\n\n-> If TE a (For all s (aRs)), a E A and s E S. S is subset of A, then a is the lower bound of S\n\nLeast Upper Bound (LUB): the least of all the upper bounds of a subset S. Denoted by VA. join of a and b\nGreatest Lower Bound (GLB) : the greatest of all the lower bounds of a subset S. Denotedy by Inverted VA. meet of a and b.\n\n-> A LUB doesn't exist for a subset, if there are no upper bounds at all or multiple minimal upper bounds exist.\n-> A GLB doesn't exist for a subset, if there are no lower bounds at all or multiple maximal lower bounds exist.\n\n-> LUB of two elements a and b. join of a and b. a V b. is the \"unique first meeting point in upward direction\"\n-> GLB of two elements a and b. meet of a and b. a n b. is the \"unique first meeting point in downward direction\".\n\nHasse Diagram of a TOSET is a straight line with greatest and smallest elements always present.\n\nWell-Ordered Set: is a TOSET but has the least element. Least element won't exist in a set, if it is infinite in the left/negative direction. eg: (Z, ⪯)\n\n-> Cardinality of POR from Hasse diagram: Number of reflexive pairs + For each path (n * (n-1) / 2) - (number of paths ending at each maximal element - 1)\n-> given n elements in base set, TOSETS give the biggest possible cardinality of a POR whose cardinality is n * (n+1) / 2. The identity relation gives the smallest POR whose cardinality is n.\n\n-> A relation on empty set satisfies all relation types.\n-> Empty relation on a non-empty set doesn't satisfy only reflexive property.\n\n\n\nLattice:\n\n-> A lattice is a POSET in which every pair of elements have GLB and LUB. So, the Greatest and Smallest elements always exist in a lattice with FINITE base set. or the relation forms a WOS on both directions.\n-> The easiest way to check if a POSET is a lattice, is by drawing a hasse diagram and check only for those pairs\n    which are in different paths. i.e. check only incomparable pairs. \n\nProperties of Lattices:\n\n-> a V a = a  ;  a n a = a;  (Idempotency)\n-> a V b = b V a  ;  a n b = b n a (Commutative)\n-> a V (b V c) = (a V b) V c  ;  a n (b n c) = (a n b) n c  (Associative)\n-> a V (a n b) = a  ;  a n (a V b) = a  (Absorption)\n-> (a n b) ⪯ {a, b} ⪯ (a V b)\n-> (a ⪯ b) iff a n b = a\n-> (a ⪯ b) iff a V b = b\n\nEvery lattice satisfy the above properties.\n\n\nSub lattices:\n\n-> Consider a POSET, You can remove any number of elements from the base set, And the remaining elements with the POR, will still be a POSET. But you cannot guarantee this if an element from POR itself is removed.\n    i.e. If S is a subset of the base set A, then (S, POR) will still remain a POSET.\n\n-> Consider a Lattice with base set A and POR R, a subset of A, S with the same relation R will still be a lattice. But it is called a SUBLATTICE iff every pair of elements in the S have SAME GLB AND ULB as the orginial Lattice.\n\n-> So, if you are thinking to form a sublattice with a subset of elements, make sure to add the GLB and ULB of every pair of elements in the subset to S.\n\n\n\nTypes of Lattices:\n\nIdentity element is defined for each operation. An element is identity element of an operation if you always get the operand when you use an operator with identity element.\n\n1) Bounded Lattice: A lattice is bounded iff it has a minimum element and a maximum element. These are \n    denoted by 0 and 1.\n\n    - a n 1 = a\n    - a V 0 = a\n\n    i.e. Bounded Lattice satisfies identity property on Meet operation where the identity element is 1 and on Join operation where the identity element is 0\n    - Every finite lattice is a bounded lattice. Some infinite lattices are also bounded, eg: ([0, 1], <=), (Power set of N, subset relation), (W, |)\n\n    - Bounded Lattice also satisfies Domination law:\n      a V 1 = 1\n      a n 0 = 0\n\n\n2) Complemented Lattice: A lattice is complemented iff every element of the base set has atleast one complement to it.\n\n    - In a POR, compliment of a is b, iff a V b = G  and  a n b = L.\n    - So, a complement lattice must also be a Bounded lattice.\n    - A TOSET with more than 2 elements is not a complemented lattice.\n    - 0 and 1 can be same in bounded lattice, then TOSET with 1 element is a complemented lattice\n\n\n3) Distributive Lattice: A lattice is distributive lattice iff for all a,b,c E base set, a,b,c satisfy the distributive property.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    -> The left lattice is called Kite/Diamond/M3 Lattice and the right lattice is Pentagon/N5 lattice. These two are NOT DISTRIBUTIVE.\n    -> A lattice is distributive iff there is no kite or pentagon as a sublattice.\n\n    Distributive Lattice  =>  At most one compliment for every element.\n                                        the contrapositive is : \n    If some element in a lattice has more than one complement  =>  Not Distributive lattice.\n    \n    distributive complemented lattice  =>  exactly one complement for every element\n\n    REMEMBER, THE ABOVE ARE ONE WAY THEOREMS",
